FrameCryptor: receiver anti-replay window, full 32-bit sender counter, parse
after RBSP unescaping (P12, frame E2EE anti-replay)

Normative design: WIRE_SPEC section 11 ("Frame E2EE wire format and receiver
replay window") and the P12 design. Applies on top of P1/P2/P3/P6/P7 (same two
files as P1-P3; the hunks do not overlap with them). Touches only
api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer.{h,cc}, its unittest and api/crypto/BUILD.gn
(test deps). No new exported API: no JNI / ObjC change, the FrameCryptionState
enum keeps its 7 values, ParticipantKeyHandler is not RTC_EXPORT.

Why: an SFU (or an attacker that controls signalling) can re-packetize any
authenticated frame and send it again, or reflect a 1:1 peer's own frames back
to it. SRTP replay protection only covers one hop and the frame AEAD key slot
stays installed, so the receiver accepted every such frame.

What changes (all in api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer.{h,cc}):
- IV: w3 = (rtpTimestamp - counter) mod 2^32 with a FULL 32-bit counter (it was
  `send_count % 0xFFFF`, a per-transformer counter with a random first value
  that never survived into the first IV). Byte layout, AAD, trailer and key
  derivation are unchanged; a frame with counter 0 is byte-identical to before.
- The sender counter lives in ParticipantKeyHandler (NextSendCounter(ssrc),
  mutex-guarded, per ssrc): it survives re-creation of the transformer, is never
  reset (key switch, enable toggle, slot change), never copied by Clone(), and
  never wraps - after 0xFFFFFFFF has been issued the frame is dropped and the
  transformer reports kEncryptionFailed once (edge-triggered).
- Receiver window: RFC 4303-style 256-frame sliding bitmap, one window per IV
  ssrc per ParticipantKeyHandler (shared by every transformer bound to the same
  participant), at most 64 windows (the 65th new stream is rejected - never
  evicted). Replay identity = (handler, IV word 1, (word 2 - word 3) mod 2^32);
  RTP metadata is never used. The check is split: ReplayCheck(commit=false) is a
  stateless pre-check before AES, ReplayCheck(commit=true) is the authoritative
  check-and-set after the GCM tag verified, so a forger can neither advance a
  window nor allocate one.
- Reflection guard: a frame whose IV ssrc is a local send ssrc of the same
  handler is rejected (1:1 shared handlers; a no-op in group calls).
- A replay verdict (duplicate, too old, reflected, cap) is a SILENT drop: no
  FrameCryptionState change and no observer callback, so it can never produce
  kDecryptionFailed / kMissingKey (which drive media_key_nack and PLI). Internal
  atomic counters per handler and one rate-limited log line per transformer
  (at most one per 10 s, only when a counter moved):
  "Q-AUDION frame-replay drop dup=N old=N refl=N cap=N kind=audio|video"
  (counts and media kind only: no ssrc, participant id, key index, key or IV).
  That literal is the build marker m150/gates.sh G3 checks.
- Window GC tied to keys: SetKeyFromMaterial compares the new material with the
  slot's current material in constant time (CRYPTO_memcmp). A different key (new
  key, zeroed retirement, short/refused material, overwrite) clears the slot bit
  from every window and erases the windows left without a slot; identical
  material (idempotent re-install) changes nothing, because resetting there
  would re-open replay. Frames a collected window accepted were sealed under a
  key that is no longer installed, so a replay of them fails the tag.
- Latent upstream bug fixed because the receive path is rewritten anyway:
  decryptFrame read the 2-byte trailer and the 12-byte IV from the RBSP-ESCAPED
  buffer. For H.264/H.265 the sender escapes ciphertext||tag||iv||trailer as one
  unit, so whenever the IV or trailer contains 00 00 0x (x <= 3) the receiver
  read a shifted IV and the frame never decrypted. The body is now unescaped
  first and trailer and IV are parsed from the unescaped tail (the desktop JS
  engine already did this).
- KeyProviderOptions: the copy constructor now copies
  discard_frame_when_cryptor_not_ready (DefaultKeyProviderImpl takes the options
  by value, so the flag the app set used to be lost: the copy carried an
  indeterminate value) and the field has the in-class default `true`.
- The dead CreateRandomNonZeroId() line in makeIv is gone with the old counter.

Tests (api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer_unittest.cc, built into
rtc_unittests, run by m150/ci/run-tests.sh as T7-T9 in both GN configs):
- the 7 upstream gtests (never run before) move to the 32-byte keys P1 requires;
- FrameReplayWindow.*: window logic (in order, reorder 255 ok / 256 too old,
  duplicate, shift >= 256, shift 255, top = 0xFFFFFFFF, per-ssrc windows, cap 64,
  GC on material change, no GC on identical material, reflection, counters);
- FrameCryptorReplay.*: real FrameCryptorTransformer pairs with mock
  transformable audio / VP8 / H.264 frames (round trip + IV layout, duplicate
  dropped with no failure state, second receiver of the same participant,
  reflection in shared-key mode, counter continuity across a destroyed sender,
  exhaustion, forged frames neither advance nor create a window, H.264 frame
  whose IV contains 00 00 01);
- FrameCryptorKat.*: the shared KAT (m150/kat/group-calls-v2-frame-crypto.json,
  sha256-pinned by m150/ci/kat2inc.py, compiled in through the generated
  api/crypto/frame_crypto_kat_vectors.inc) runs through the real receiver and
  sender transformers: every case, counterCases (counter seam), ringScenarios
  and replayScenarios - closing review item L3 (no test ever ran the native
  decryptFrame over any vector).


